INT. FIELD KITCHEN IN A BOMBED BREWERY - NIGHT

Copper vats, split and blackened, loom in the dark like wrecked bells. A tarp stretched across a hole in the roof sags with rain. Sacks of flour stacked against a wall. A single iron stove glows red. The smell: cabbage, wet wool, old hops.

MAGNUSSEN, 40s, a sergeant with a heavy gray mustache, ladles stew into tin mugs, one at a time, with exaggerated care. CORPORAL PETIT, 20s, a slight boy in a too-large helmet, sits on an upturned crate, cradling a tin plate. WOJCIK, 30s, a private with a broken front tooth, whittles a stick by the stove. GREENE, 20s, a radio operator, sits cross-legged by a silent set. DELACROIX, 30s, a medic with a stained armband, mends a sleeve with a curved needle.

Nobody speaks for a long time. Rain on the tarp.

MAGNUSSEN
Eat.

PETIT
I'm not hungry.

MAGNUSSEN
Eat anyway.

PETIT
It's the cabbage.

MAGNUSSEN
It's what we have.

WOJCIK
(not looking up)
My mother would've cried at that stew.

GREENE
From pride?

WOJCIK
From shame.

Delacroix snorts. The needle slips. She sucks her thumb, swears softly in French.

DELACROIX
Pardon.

MAGNUSSEN
No need.

PETIT
(quietly)
What's that noise?

MAGNUSSEN
What noise?

PETIT
Under the rain. Like a drum.

They all listen. A distant, regular thudding. Far off. Nearly a heartbeat.

GREENE
Artillery.

WOJCIK
Ours?

GREENE
Doesn't matter.

MAGNUSSEN
It matters.

GREENE
It's three miles off.

MAGNUSSEN
Then it's three miles off.

He sets down the ladle. Wipes his hands. Takes his own mug and sits on a sack of flour. The stove ticks.

WOJCIK
Sergeant.

MAGNUSSEN
Mm.

WOJCIK
Did this place make good beer?

MAGNUSSEN
The best in the province.

WOJCIK
How do you know?

MAGNUSSEN
My father used to drink it.

PETIT
(overlapping)
Did he --

DELACROIX
(overlapping)
Is that why --

GREENE
(overlapping)
Sergeant, is that why we --

MAGNUSSEN
(raising a hand)
One at a time.

A beat. Greene falls silent. Petit looks down. Delacroix threads her needle. Wojcik scrapes the stick.

MAGNUSSEN (CONT'D)
It's why I asked to cook here.

Silence. The rain intensifies. The tarp bellies and drips.

Wojcik whittles. A curl of wood falls to the floor. He blows the dust from the stick, and holds it up: a tiny, crude beer mug.

He sets it on the sack beside Magnussen. Magnussen looks at it. He doesn't say a thing. He picks it up, turns it in the light of the stove, and tucks it into his breast pocket.

The distant thudding does not stop. Petit finishes his stew.
